Raw dev notes -- The Unsung Hero: Hardening a WhatsApp AI Bot

**Project Background:**
This project is a WhatsApp bot designed to bring the power of an AI knowledge base (think something like NotebookLM) directly to users' phones. The idea is to make information retrieval super accessible – just ask a question in WhatsApp, and the bot taps into its knowledge sources to give you an answer, complete with citations. It's all about democratizing access to structured information.

**What was done in this period:**
Man, this past week or so has been a deep dive into the less glamorous but absolutely critical world of hardening and reliability. It felt like 80% of my time was spent shoring up defenses and making things *actually* production-ready.

A huge chunk of work went into making the bot resilient to all sorts of weird inputs. I spent ages hardening the parse layer against malformed WhatsApp payloads – turns out people (and the API itself sometimes) send all sorts of junk. This included guarding against non-dictionary contexts, reactions, and interactive messages, which apparently Fable (my internal test harness/persona) found a way to trigger.

The citation system got a massive overhaul. I fixed the RTL (right-to-left) rendering for Hebrew sources, which was a real pain to get right. More importantly, I dug deep into how citations were being generated by the AI. There was a lot of "batch-noise" and AI-summary boilerplate getting mixed in, making the sources look messy or even misleading. I implemented logic to drop that noise, mine the `cited_text` for *real* source links, and rework how URL-bearing citations are labeled. The goal was to make every citation traceable and trustworthy, preventing ambiguous links or linking to the wrong source entirely.

Operational stability was another big focus. I set up a daily watcher for WhatsApp template approvals, added a Kapso (our messaging provider) message-usage monitor with threshold alerting, and even aligned its billing reset. We also migrated our ingress from a `cloudflared` quick-tunnel to a more stable `ngrok` static domain, which was a bit of a lift. And, crucially, I built an end-to-end health check watchdog – not just checking if processes are running, but if the whole system is actually responding.

Finally, there was a lot of reconciliation work to prevent duplicate messages and ensure idempotency. This involved a "durable handled-set" to stop short-answer re-sends, which is trickier than it sounds. And on the privacy front, I added explicit confirmations for operator, retention, and disclaimers, along with setting up the privacy policy URLs.

**What was hard or surprising:**
The absolute nastiest bug was the "webhook body-cap consumed the stream" issue. I was trying to read the raw request body for some logging/debugging, but `request.json` (from Flask, I think?) was consuming the input stream, leaving nothing for `json.loads(raw_body)`. It took me way too long to realize that the stream can only be read once. Classic "read-once" problem, always catches me out.

The citation rendering was also surprisingly complex. The AI's output isn't always perfectly structured, and trying to reliably extract and present source links, especially when there were "per-line tagging leaks" into the source text, felt like a constant battle against regex and string manipulation. Getting the RTL right for Hebrew was a specific, fiddly challenge.

And the sheer volume of "hardening" commits really highlighted how much work goes into making something truly robust. It's not just about getting the feature to work, but making it work *every single time*, no matter what garbage gets thrown at it.

**Personal reflection:**
This period felt less like building shiny new features and more like being a digital plumber, fixing leaks and shoring up foundations. Honestly, it's not always the most exciting work, but there's a deep satisfaction in knowing the system is more resilient, more trustworthy, and less likely to blow up in production. It really hammered home the importance of defensive programming and anticipating all the ways things can go wrong. I learned a ton about the nuances of API integrations, especially with external services like WhatsApp and our messaging provider. The "webhook body-cap" bug was a painful but valuable lesson in HTTP request handling. It feels like I'm growing past just "making it work" to "making it *unbreakable*."

**What's next:**
Time to tackle some of those documented backlog decisions, maybe demote voice and inline-health for now.
